<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rupa Goswami</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1489–<a href="/years/1564">1564</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rupa Goswami was a theologian and poet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaudiya-vaishnavism">Gaudiya Vaishnavism</a>, one of the six Goswamis of Vrindavan. With his brother Sanatana he served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-husain-shah">Alauddin Husain Shah</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> as a high official before leaving to follow Chaitanya, who sent him to Vrindavan. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> works, especially the Bhakti-rasamrita-sindhu, set out the theory of devotion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> for the movement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rupa-goswami.jpg" alt="Rupa Goswami" width="282" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: HareKrishnaPhotos · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rupa_Goswami.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rupa Goswami was born at Fateyabad in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> into a family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> origin. He was the younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanatana-goswami">Sanatana Goswami</a> and the elder brother of Anupama; the three were born Santosha, Amara and Vallabha. The brothers moved to Ramakeli, where Sanatana and Rupa served in the government of the sultan Ḥusayn Šāh under the titles Sakar Mallik and Dabir Khas. While still at court they began to write to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaitanya-mahaprabhu">Chaitanya Mahaprabhu</a>, and when he passed through Ramakeli on his way to Vrindavan they hurried to meet him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The brothers gave up their high positions, and Chaitanya initiated them into the ascetic life under the names by which they are known. On his way back from Vraja he told Sanatana and Rupa to go to Vrindavan, study the devotional scriptures, recover lost sacred sites and make their greatness known. They found Vrindavan wooded and empty of people, and set about locating the holy places of Braj by studying the Puranic geography of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>'s life, while also developing stories of their own. Chaitanya later sent more devotees, and so formed the Six Goswamis, a group led by the two brothers that wrote a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> literature on theology, ritual, grammar and literary theory.</p><p>Rupa is linked with a site east of Nandīśvara and with Sevakunj in Vrindavan, and he discovered the image of Govindadeva. His possessions passed after his death to his nephew and disciple Jiva Goswami, the son of Anupama.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The Bhakti-rasāmṛta-sindhu traces devotion from faith, its lowest stage, to the highest ecstasy of love. Its sequel, the Ujjvala-nilamani, treats divine conjugal love. Rupa began the dramas Vidagdhamadhava and Lalitamadhava as one work in 1516 and finished them as two, in 1524 and 1529, after a vision in which Satyabhama is said to have told him to divide them. He also wrote the one-act play Danakelikaumudi in 1549, a manual for the worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> at Janmastami, rules for drama, and the Upadesamrta, eleven verses of instruction.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rupa Goswami” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Husain Shah employed Hindus in high office, among them the brothers <b>Rupa</b> and Sanatana, who later became leading theologians of the Gaudiya Vaishnava movement of Chaitanya.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Vrindavan, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=27.57,77.69&amp;year=1537" aria-label="Vrindavan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6590 1153 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Rupa Goswami"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7040 1423h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7040 1423h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=27.57,77.69&amp;year=1537">What was here in 1537? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
